WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.077
Membership tier has a documented business order, so an ordinal encoder can preserve that rank.

00:00:06.077 --> 00:00:08.992
The category list is written explicitly.

00:00:08.992 --> 00:00:14.697
That matters because software should not guess the intended order from alphabetical sorting.

00:00:14.697 --> 00:00:18.027
The resulting codes are compact and ordered.

00:00:18.027 --> 00:00:23.532
But remember the earlier caution: some models may treat the numeric gaps as equal,

00:00:23.532 --> 00:00:27.452
so the representation must fit the method and interpretation.

00:00:27.452 --> 00:00:33.862
The easiest way to classify a variable is to ask what statements are meaningful in the real world.

00:00:33.862 --> 00:00:42.082
Can values only be different, can they be ordered, are differences meaningful, and does zero mean absence?

00:00:42.082 --> 00:00:48.077
Those questions are more reliable than looking at whether the software stores the column as an integer or

00:00:48.077 --> 00:00:49.495
floating-point number.
